@* Badge: a small marker for a fact next to a value.

   A pill with no border, which is the whole of its contract with StatusLabel: the two vocabularies
   are read side by side on a delivery row - a status and an overdue mark - and the design keeps
   them apart by shape rather than by hue, so a badge never takes square corners and a status label
   never loses them.

   The overdue face carries the warning mark as well as the tint, for the reason every status label
   carries a shape: colour is never the only signal. The role face carries nothing but its word,
   because a role is not a warning. *@

<span class="dt-badge @Modifier @Class">
    @if (Variant == DtBadgeVariant.Overdue)
    {
        <Icon Name="IconName.Warning" />
    }
    @ChildContent
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>Which of the three looks to wear.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public DtBadgeVariant Variant { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The screen's own class on this badge: a hook a test can name, or <c>dt-muted</c> for the
    /// off state of a pair - which is how the design draws "off duty" beside "on duty" without
    /// giving the absence of something a colour of its own.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The fact, already localized by the screen that states it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The modifier class the variant wears. <see cref="DtBadgeVariant.Neutral"/> adds none: the
    /// base class is already the neutral pill, and an empty modifier keeps the rendered attribute
    /// identical to what a screen would have written by hand.
    /// </summary>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentOutOfRangeException">
    /// A variant with no class. Total over the enum, so a fourth added without a decision about how
    /// it looks fails here rather than rendering as a neutral pill and being mistaken for one.
    /// </exception>
    private string Modifier => Variant switch
    {
        DtBadgeVariant.Neutral => string.Empty,
        DtBadgeVariant.Overdue => "dt-badge--overdue",
        DtBadgeVariant.Role => "dt-badge--role",
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
            nameof(Variant),
            Variant,
            "No badge class is defined for this variant."),
    };
}
